\begin{abstract}
    \textbf{Motivation:}
    Cardiac segmentation is a fundamental step in cardiac image analysis.
    However, the annotation of cardiac segmentation is labor-intensive and time-consuming.
    To alleviate the annotation burden, we propose a novel framework, Dual-Teacher, which integrates intra-domain and inter-domain teachers to learn from unlabeled data.
    \textbf{Method:}
    We first train an intra-domain teacher to learn from unlabeled data.
    Then, we train an inter-domain teacher to learn from the pseudo-labeled data generated by the intra-domain teacher.
    Finally, we train a student network to learn from the pseudo-labeled data generated by the inter-domain teacher.
    \textbf{Results:}
    We evaluate our method on the public dataset, the 2017 ACDC Challenge dataset.
    The experimental results show that our method outperforms the state-of-the-art methods.
    \textbf{Conclusion:}
    Our method can effectively alleviate the annotation burden of cardiac segmentation.
\end{abstract}

\end{document}

