\textbf{Abstract:}
B\"uchi's decidability theorem is a fundamental result in the theory of formal languages. It states that the emptiness problem for regular languages is decidable. The theorem is usually proved by showing that the emptiness problem for regular languages is equivalent to the halting problem for Turing machines. In this paper, we show that the halting problem for Turing machines is equivalent to the emptiness problem for regular languages. This result is surprising because it is well known that the halting problem for Turing machines is undecidable.
\end{quote}

