This study aims at understanding the effect of game and power dynamics such as momentum, velocity and strength on gaming experiences with low bandwidth wireless-power devices like cellphones and wearable sensors (e.g., gyroscopes). We investigate various aspects affecting mobile device performance through modeling the interactions between two kinds of games:'realistic' games played by highly skilled gamers versus games which are more 'ludic' or challenging but also involve non-player character (NPC) players. Realistic, competitive games are characterized, among other things, by relative player intelligence and difficulty – the latter being inversely related to perceived player skill. Our results show that it is possible to make a powerful, well designed immersive mobile power experience even without considering any advanced knowledge of microelectronics and RF technology, provided one uses the right control scheme. In contrast, ludic games play requires expert knowledge about the interaction mechanics based on complex relationships between game components and gameplay mechanics that require great care in terms of design choices regarding electromagnetic coupling properties within the interface. Furthermore, we analyze how game development can foster innovation leading to improved user interfaces within these types of hybrid multiplayer games – such as Pokemon GO and Angry Birds Go – by using low bandwidth, passive power.

In this way the author has tested "WiFi Games." The findings, as indicated above (if not described here completely), include

Ludicrous performance of WiFi-based smartphones which are less capable than their wired counterparts (or more powerful ones) due to the lack of high signal efficiency technologies used on both sides;

Problems observed with lag time associated with WiFi charging;

Lack of reliable feedback provided by the game itself: the ability to interact while playing – especially with non-player characters (NPCs) to help out players whose hands and the mouse/joystick combination are overloaded; especially when compared with a Bluetooth-connected computer;

Disappointing gameplay when running on cellular data;

Lowest quality audio output during wireless calls while listening to music on speakerphone;

Lack of robustness of user interface for smartphone/tablet users, despite efforts of designers who have spent years optimizing usability within this category; some of them only use 2 buttons for navigation and communication.

