A 4D multi-label system, where labels, not just points or lines can be aligned to form an object shape (MIMO), has been described in previous work [12]. In this contribution we present a new and more powerful method to construct images that can capture objects with many different scales at once. To address this issue, our setup makes use of a large number of temporal images corresponding to different positions on each face of the human subject collected over several years during several experiments. Using one image per subject in the set allows us to generate a single large-scale image representing a user's entire face. The resulting 4D reconstruction system consists of four components—one at every point along each edge of the subject's facial segment (Fig. 1):

a cylindrical projection component, comprising a camera tracking component whose distance from the facial skin surface is fixed;

an alignment component, whose focal length and orientation are variable across trials according to a pre-determined objective function;

several temporal imaging components corresponding to different visual landmarks throughout such time slices along which we wish to apply different alignment and scale parameters to the alignment component; and

a color matching component based on the linear-color relationship between the orientation of the alignment component and the focal plane of the three other component parts in a given frame.

While these components can act independently in real-time by their individual locations along each axis of one face of the subject, our focus is on merging them into a coherent whole before the eye gaze direction changes so as to better accommodate all eye features through the integration phase. Specifically, as long as the orientation at a particular point in the intersection between adjacent labels remains unchanged after the 3-dimensional mapping project, then all labeling errors will be reduced relative to a rigid alignment scheme. The combination model allows to merge two sets of orthographic views as well as several separate sets of viewpoints simultaneously by simply allowing them to cross paths via arbitrary projections that do not need to be directed towards the same point in space. By making use of the fact that projected labels tend to align precisely along contiguously projected axes across multiple dimensions, they also have a low effective size. Our implementation relies upon a fast parallel algorithm that efficiently handles the computations associated with the 4-component alignment scheme while maintaining a sufficiently dense data structure that avoids any local sub

